From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:31:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 22/36] feat(deacon): detect Dolt auto-push outages via
 push-state divergence (gcp-qhx1)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 2026-08-12 winnow outage ran ~18h with a broken dolt auto-push and nothing
in the city noticing. Two independent causes; this commit fixes the second.

`bd` DOES warn when auto-push fails, but only when it is neither quiet nor
--json — and gc drives every bead WRITE through beads with exactly that flag,
so the caller class performing essentially all writes is precisely the class
that cannot see the warning. That half is upstream gastownhall/beads and is out
of scope. The half fixed here is that no patrol covered it.

The detector is a new pack script wired into mol-deacon-patrol as a
`dolt-push-divergence` step after `dolt-health`. It deliberately does NOT go
through `gc dolt health`: that command observes the MANAGED city server only,
and the rig that went dark is pinned to an EXPLICIT endpoint (:3307, not the
managed :51160), so a health-routed check is structurally blind to exactly the
case being caught. Every scope is reached through `gc bd sql -C <scope path>`,
which resolves that scope's own .beads/config.yaml — managed, inherited and
explicit alike.

The hard part is the false positive, and it drove the whole design. Auto-push
debounces on `dolt.auto-push-interval`, so on a perfectly HEALTHY store
`last_commit` trails head for up to a full interval on every sawtooth trough —
a measured healthy trace shows it trailing for five consecutive minutes. A
naive "alert when last_commit != head" check fires there. So the signal is not
divergence but divergence that FAILS TO CONVERGE, and the measured quantity is
the age of the OLDEST UNPUSHED COMMIT: bounded by one interval on a healthy
store, unbounded during an outage. Alert at >= 3x the interval, read per-scope
from config rather than hardcoded. Measuring the oldest unpushed commit rather
than "how long since last_commit was written" is also what keeps a long-idle
store from false-positiving when its first new commit lands.

Wired as deacon `pre_start` because pack assets install into a content-hashed
cache directory a formula step cannot name; {{.ConfigDir}} is the only stable
handle (same constraint as the witness worktree reaper). --snapshot records the
script's own resolved path so the patrol step re-runs the sweep each cycle
instead of reading a session-start measurement.

Validation: 9 new assertions in gastown/tests/test_dolt_push_state_check.sh,
including the bead's measured 14:08-14:15 healthy trough replayed sample by
sample (all 8 stay OK), the 18h-frozen outage shape, exact threshold boundary
(899s OK / 900s STALE), config-driven interval in both flat and nested YAML
spellings, explicit-endpoint coverage asserted via the recorded argv, and
UNKNOWN-not-OK for unreadable stores. Also verified live: the check fired on a
real in-progress winnow outage, crossing OK(890s) -> STALE(924s) at the
threshold and naming the frozen commit (filed as gcp-02bm).

dolt-remotes-patrol and `gc dolt sync` are untouched, as the bead requires; a
contract test in test_gastown_pack_assets.sh pins that along with the wiring.

Escalation mails mayor once per outage per scope and nudges thereafter, since a
real outage stays STALE across many patrol cycles and a mail per cycle would
both bury the mayor and commit a bead per message.

Pre-existing test failures on the fork point (bare-bd guard plus gascity/
discord/github suites) are unrelated and filed as gcp-gsif; this branch's
failure set is a strict subset of the fork point's.
